542. 01 Matrix

- LeetCode: https://leetcode.com/problems/01-matrix/
- Topic: Graphs, BFS, Multi-Source BFS, Matrix
- Language: Java
- Status: Solved ✅

1. My Intuition

Initially, I thought of iterating through every cell and performing BFS from each cell containing "1" until I encountered a "0".

However, this would result in repeated BFS traversals.

Instead, I realized that I could use Multi-Source BFS by inserting all cells containing "0" into the queue simultaneously.

Since all zero cells have distance "0", BFS can expand from all of them level by level and calculate the shortest distance of every cell to its nearest zero.

2. My Approach

1. Create a distance matrix "dist" of the same dimensions as the input matrix.
2. Initialize the queue.
3. Iterate through the entire matrix:
   - If the cell contains "0", assign distance "0" and insert its coordinates into the queue.
   - Otherwise, initialize its distance as "-1", representing an unvisited cell.
4. Define four direction arrays to explore up, down, left, and right.
5. Perform Multi-Source BFS:
   - Remove the current cell from the queue.
   - Explore its four neighbours.
   - Check whether the neighbour is within the matrix boundaries and unvisited.
   - Assign its distance as "dist[r][c] + 1".
   - Insert the neighbour into the queue.
6. Continue until the queue becomes empty.
7. Return the distance matrix.

3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

My initial approach was to start a separate BFS from every cell containing "1".

This would repeatedly explore the same cells and increase the time complexity.

What I corrected: Instead of starting BFS separately for every cell, I inserted all zero cells into the queue initially and performed a single Multi-Source BFS.

4. What Clicked

- When multiple starting points exist, I can consider Multi-Source BFS.
- All source cells are inserted into the queue initially with distance "0".
- BFS explores cells level by level.
- The first time an unvisited cell is reached, its shortest distance is determined.
- Using "-1" in the distance matrix helps identify unvisited cells without requiring a separate visited array.
- Direction arrays simplify neighbour exploration.

Key concept: Multi-Source BFS calculates the shortest distance from every cell to its nearest source in a single traversal.

5. Final Submitted Code

class Solution {
    public int[][] updateMatrix(int[][] mat) {

        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;

        int[][] dist = new int[m][n];

        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (mat[i][j] == 0) {
                    dist[i][j] = 0;
                    q.offer(new int[]{i, j});
                } else {
                    dist[i][j] = -1;
                }
            }
        }

        int[] dr = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int[] dc = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int[] curr = q.poll();

            int r = curr[0];
            int c = curr[1];

            for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {

                int nr = r + dr[k];
                int nc = c + dc[k];

                if (nr >= 0 && nr < m &&
                    nc >= 0 && nc < n &&
                    dist[nr][nc] == -1) {

                    dist[nr][nc] = dist[r][c] + 1;

                    q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }

        return dist;
    }
}

6. Complexity Analysis

Let "m" be the number of rows and "n" be the number of columns.

- Time Complexity: "O(m × n)" — Every cell is inserted into and processed from the queue at most once. Each cell checks four neighbours.
- Space Complexity: "O(m × n)" — Distance matrix and queue.

7. Revision Notes

- Identify all source cells first.
- Insert every source into the queue with distance "0".
- Use BFS to expand level by level.
- Use a distance matrix to track visited cells and shortest distances.
- Apply Multi-Source BFS when finding the nearest source among multiple starting points.
- Remember: First visit in unweighted BFS guarantees the shortest distance.
